{% commandanatomy
   command="read"
   subcommand="summary"
   input="--dir raw_reads/"
   option="--output read_summary"
   optionSub="optional" /%}

`segul read summary` calculates summary statistics for raw sequence reads in FASTQ format. For assembled contigs, see [Genomic Contig Summary](/docs/cli-usage/contig-summary/).

{% aside type="note" %}
The `--phred`, `--dup-size`, and `--plot` options, the extra statistics, and the Complete mode duplication, overrepresented sequence, and adapter results are available in SEGUL v0.25.0 and later. Check the [Installation Guide](/docs/installation/update/#segul-cli) to update SEGUL.
{% /aside %}

## Input formats

- Gunzip Compressed FASTQ (.fq.gz/.fastq.gz), including BGZF
- Standard FASTQ (.fq/.fastq)

SEGUL detects the compression from the file content. Each file is read once and summarized on all CPU cores, and BGZF files are also decompressed in parallel.

## Usage

```bash
segul read summary -i raw_reads.fq.gz
```

or using directory input:

```bash
segul read summary -d /raw_read_dir
```

The output file will be saved as `default-read-summary.csv` in `Read-Summary` directory.

To specify the output directory, use the `--output` or `-o` argument:

```bash
segul read summary -d /raw_read_dir -o read_dir_output
```

You can also use the `--prefix` argument to specify the prefix name of the output files:

```bash
segul read summary -d /raw_read_dir -o read_dir_output --prefix my_read_summary
```

## Summary modes

SEGUL provides three modes to calculate summary statistics for raw reads:

- **Minimal**: read count only
- **Default**: generate essential statistics (see below)
- **Complete**: generate all the essential statistics plus statistics per read position, distributions, duplication, overrepresented sequences, and adapter content (see below).

Use the `--mode` option to choose `minimal`, `default`, or `complete`:

```bash
segul read summary -d /raw_read_dir --mode complete
```

## Statistics

Essential statistics for raw reads:

- Number of reads
- Number of bases
- Mean read length
- Minimum read length
- Maximum read length
- GC count
- GC content
- AT count
- AT content
- A, C, G, T, N count
- Low-quality base count
- Mean base quality
- Min base quality
- Max base quality
- Median, standard deviation, and N50 of read length
- Bases with quality 20 or higher (Q20) and 30 or higher (Q30)
- Mean quality per read, and reads with mean quality below 20
- Quality score offset (Phred+33 or Phred+64)

Complete mode adds:

- Quality quartiles, 10th and 90th percentiles, and base composition, including N, at each read position (`read-pos-summary.zip`)
- Read length, mean read quality, and GC content distributions, with the theoretical GC distribution (`read-dist-summary.zip`)
- Duplication across every read of each file, with the number of copies of each sequence (see below)
- Overrepresented sequences, above 0.1% of all reads, with the adapter they match. Reads longer than 75 bp are compared by their first 50 bp, like FastQC
- Adapter content by position for Illumina Universal, Nextera, Illumina Small RNA, poly-A, and poly-G
- All of the above in `read-report.json`, which SEGUI opens as an interactive report. Open it in the terminal with [`segul view summary`](/docs/cli-usage/view-summary/)

## Comparison with FastQC and fastp

Complete mode covers the read quality checks of
[FastQC](https://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/projects/fastqc/) and
[fastp](https://github.com/OpenGene/fastp), and adds options only SEGUL has. fastp also trims and
filters reads, which SEGUL does not.

| Feature | SEGUL | FastQC | fastp |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Read and base counts, GC content | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Q20 and Q30 bases | ✓ | — | ✓ |
| Read length N50 | ✓ | — | — |
| Quality by position | Quartiles | Quartiles | Mean |
| Base composition and N content by position | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Mean read quality distribution | ✓ | ✓ | — |
| GC distribution with theoretical curve | ✓ | ✓ | — |
| Read length distribution | ✓ | ✓ | — |
| Duplication | Every read, exact with `--dup-size 0` | First 100,000 distinct sequences | Every read, hashed estimate |
| Overrepresented sequences | Every read | First 100,000 distinct sequences | Sampled reads |
| Adapter content by position | ✓ | ✓ | Detected for trimming |
| Pass, warn, and fail checks | ✓ | ✓ | — |
| Per-tile quality, k-mer content | — | ✓ | k-mer counts |
| Insert size of paired reads | — | — | ✓ |
| Many samples in one table | ✓ | — | — |
| Charts | PNG, SVG, or PDF; SEGUI and terminal viewers | HTML report | HTML report |
| Read trimming and filtering | — | — | ✓ |

The pass, warn, and fail checks follow FastQC conventions. SEGUL also checks Q30 bases,
which FastQC does not.

## Duplication

SEGUL compares the full sequence of every read in the file. FastQC follows only the first 100,000 distinct sequences, which can misjudge long or sorted files.

Counting every distinct sequence exactly takes a lot of memory. By default, SEGUL keeps a uniform sample of 100,000 distinct sequences and counts their copies across all reads:

- The duplicate share is estimated with an error of about 0.3% of the distinct sequences.
- The levels chart uses the exact copy counts of the sample.
- Files with fewer distinct sequences are counted exactly.
- The sample uses about 30 bytes per sequence, about 3 MB per file by default.

Use `--dup-size` to change the sample size. Larger samples are more accurate, and `0` counts every distinct sequence exactly:

```bash
segul read summary -d /raw_read_dir --mode complete --dup-size 1000000
```

Duplication results are the same with any number of threads. Overrepresented sequences are found among all reads. In files with many distinct sequences, their counts can be short by up to 0.025% of reads, listed as `max_error_pct` in `read-report.json`.

## Quality score offset

SEGUL detects the quality score offset from the first reads. Use `--phred 33` or `--phred 64` to set it.

## Charts

Add `--plot` to also write charts to `<OUTPUT>/plots`. Complete mode adds charts of quality by position (`pos-qscore-box_<sample>`), read length (`length-dist`), mean read quality (`read-qscore-dist`), GC content (`gc-dist_<sample>`), N content (`pos-n-content`), adapter content (`adapter-content_<sample>`), and duplication (`duplication_<sample>`):

```bash
segul read summary -d /raw_read_dir --mode complete --plot
```

## All options

| Option                 | Description                                                    |
| ---------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `-d`, `--dir`          | Directory with FASTQ files                                     |
| `-i`, `--input`        | FASTQ files. Allows wildcards                                  |
| `-f`, `--input-format` | Input format: `auto` (default), `fastq`, or `gzip`             |
| `--mode`               | Summary mode: `default` (default), `minimal`, or `complete`    |
| `--phred`              | Quality score offset: `auto` (default), `33`, or `64`       |
| `--dup-size`           | Distinct sequences kept to estimate duplication. Default: `100000`. `0` counts all |
| `--plot`               | Also write charts to `<OUTPUT>/plots`                          |
| `-o`, `--output`       | Output directory. Default: `Read-Summary`                      |
| `--prefix`             | Prefix of the output files                                     |
| `--force`              | Overwrite an existing output directory                         |
| `--log`                | Log file path. Default: `segul.log`                            |
